<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the generated iOS bundle by Tauri, ON TOP of the sibling
  Info.plist, which both platforms share.

  Measured on 2026-09-22, because the merge is easy to mis-read: after
  `tauri ios init` the generated `gen/apple/onchato_iOS/Info.plist` carries
  NEITHER file's keys and looks broken. The merge happens at BUILD time. A key
  placed here came out in the bundle after `tauri ios build`, alongside the
  ones from Info.plist.

  This file exists for exactly one key, and the reason is a difference between
  the two Apple platforms rather than a preference.

  NSCameraUsageDescription is deliberately ABSENT from the shared Info.plist,
  and the comment there says why: on macOS, scanning a QR code goes through
  the web platform's BarcodeDetector, which no WebKit has ever shipped, so
  declaring the camera would promise a feature that still could not work.

  On iOS none of that holds. Scanning does not go through the webview at all —
  it goes through the native `tauri-plugin-barcode-scanner` (our fork, which is
  `#![cfg(mobile)]`), the same path Android takes, and the Android manifest
  declares CAMERA for the same reason. So on iOS the camera is a capability the
  app really has and really uses, and the key belongs here and only here.

  Without it the app does not merely get refused: iOS terminates a process that
  touches capture with no declared purpose. That is a crash on the first scan,
  not a denied permission.

  The string is what iOS shows in its own consent dialog, so it is written for
  the person reading that dialog, in the app's default language, and matches
  the microphone string next door in tone and in spelling without diacritics.
  A single Info.plist string cannot be localized without InfoPlist.strings
  files, which is a deliberate omission here as it is there.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
  <string>onchato uzywa aparatu tylko do zeskanowania kodu QR kontaktu. Obraz nie jest nigdzie zapisywany ani wysylany.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
